--- body ---
## Context

git-erg installs `tickets/AGENTS.md` into every adopter repo — 2 141 bytes as
shipped (`src/go/assets/AGENTS.md`, embedded and mapped by
`src/go/bootstrap_assets.go`). Adopters then wire it into the agent's *resident*
context: the harness convention is a bare `@tickets/AGENTS.md` line in the
project `CLAUDE.md`, and `erg install --inject-agents` writes a pointer into the
root `AGENTS.md`. Every byte of that file is re-read at the start of every
session, in every session of every adopter repo. Nothing budgets it: the asset
machinery is hash-based and asks one question — pristine or locally edited
(`manifest.go`, `isCleanUpgrade`) — and size is never consulted.

The 11-repo sweep of 2026-09-10 (tabulated in 0278) settles the scale of the
problem, and it is smaller than it looked: **exactly one repo has ever edited
this file.** ImperialDragonHarness stands at 8 005 bytes, 3.7x baseline, roughly
2 000 tokens paid at every session start. Every other adopter is byte-for-byte
a shipped release — six current, four merely behind (since refreshed), one stampless.

So this is not a widespread drift to be policed. It is one repo's accretion, and
the interesting fact about it is *what* accreted: optimistic-ID collision
recovery, the per-PR `gh pr view` scan, the cross-PR CI gate, artifacts versus
decision records. Almost none of that is harness-specific. It is generic git-erg
lore that had nowhere else to live, so it went into the only file the agent was
guaranteed to read.

## The decision (author, 2026-09-10)

Local editing of the shipped `tickets/AGENTS.md` is to be **forbidden**, not
merely budgeted. That is enforceable precisely because the sweep shows a single
instance — but a ban with no escape hatch would destroy the one repo's content,
which is real and worth keeping. The ban therefore comes with somewhere for the
content to go, and the choice of destination is the whole design:

- **Not** upstreamed into `assets/AGENTS.md`. Folding 5 900 bytes into the
  shipped asset makes every adopter pay ~1 500 resident tokens for war stories
  most of them will never hit. That trades one repo's cost for eleven.
- **Into the on-demand channel git-erg already has.** `erg spec` and
  `erg integration` are commands that print documentation when an agent asks
  for it. Long-form collision recovery belongs there, named by a one-line
  pointer from the shipped `AGENTS.md`. Pointer resident, payload on demand —
  the same split the harness applied to its own rules tree on 2026-09-09,
  measured at 31 000 -> 11 300 resident tokens.
- **Adopter-specific lore** (a repo's own CI job names, its local conventions)
  goes in an adopter-owned file the shipped asset points at, never in the
  shipped asset itself.

With the escape hatch in place the ban is cheap to enforce: an unrecognized hash
becomes a hard error rather than a preserved local edit.

**The table prerequisite is removed (2026-09-16).** This ticket was coupled to
0278's `extraHistoricalHashes` on the strength of one repo -- `chemin-de-voix`,
reported by the 2026-09-10 sweep as stampless and therefore certain to fail a
hard error. Measured directly on 2026-09-16, that is false: it has carried a
stamp since 2026-06-03 and both its assets match it byte for byte, so it is
recognized through the ordinary stamp path and the table is never consulted.
0281 (the table) is closed wontfix; see it for the evidence.

The coupling as originally written already named the alternative -- "or the one
stampless repo fails instead of upgrading". There is no such repo. What
survives is the weaker, honest form: **re-survey the known adopters
immediately before enforcing**, and update any that is genuinely
unrecognizable. None is known today, and the sweep covers 11 repos rather than
a provably complete list, so the check is cheap insurance rather than a
blocker.

## Relevant files

- `src/go/assets/AGENTS.md` — the shipped baseline, 2 141 bytes; must stay lean
- `src/go/bootstrap_assets.go` — embeds it; maps `tickets/AGENTS.md` -> `assets/AGENTS.md`
- `src/go/manifest.go` — `isCleanUpgrade`: where pristine-vs-edited is decided
  today, and where the ban's verdict changes
- `src/go/install.go` — `--inject-agents`, the flag that makes the file resident
- `src/go/spec.go`, `src/go/integration.go` (or their equivalents) — the
  on-demand channel the displaced content moves into
- `/home/haduong/.claude/tickets/AGENTS.md` — the single specimen; its excess
  content is the migration's input

## Actions

1. Re-survey the known adopters immediately before enforcing, and update any
   that carries an unrecognizable-but-legitimate copy (`erg init` in that
   repo). This replaces the former "land 0278 first": no adopter is known to
   need it, and the one that was said to need it does not. A snapshot taken
   weeks earlier is not evidence about the moment of enforcement -- the
   2026-09-10 sweep was wrong about `chemin-de-voix` on the day it was
   taken.
2. Partition the harness's 8 005-byte copy: what is generic git-erg lore (most
   of it), what is adopter-specific (CI job names, local paths), what is already
   in the shipped baseline.
3. Move the generic long-form material into the `erg integration` / `erg spec`
   output, and leave a one-line pointer in the shipped `AGENTS.md` naming the
   command that prints it. Keep the shipped file's resident cost within its
   current order of magnitude — a lean file is the point, not a side effect.
4. Give adopter-specific lore a declared home the shipped asset points at, so
   the ban never forces a repo to discard local knowledge.
5. Make an unrecognized hash a hard error on `erg check`, with a message that
   names the escape hatch rather than only refusing.
6. Return the harness copy to pristine and verify the lore is still reachable
   from a cold session — the migration is only done when an agent that needs the
   collision recipe can still find it.

## Test

Red step: a fixture repo whose `tickets/AGENTS.md` carries a local edit,
asserting `erg check` fails and names the escape hatch. Against today's code it
passes silently, so watch it fail before the fix.

The positive control matters as much: a fixture at a recognized shipped hash
must stay silent. A check whose all-clear cannot be told from "I could not look"
is not a check — so demonstrate the failing arm firing before trusting the
passing arm's silence.

Migration test: the collision-recovery material is reachable from `erg integration`
(or `erg spec`) output after the move, asserted on content, not on exit code.

## Verification

- [ ] `erg check` fails on a locally-edited `tickets/AGENTS.md`, message names where the content should go
- [ ] `erg check` stays silent on every hash recognized through the stamp
      path, verified by a re-survey taken at enforcement time
- [ ] the shipped `assets/AGENTS.md` has not grown by more than a pointer
- [ ] `erg integration` output contains the optimistic-ID collision recipe
- [ ] the ImperialDragonHarness copy is byte-identical to the shipped asset, and its lore is reachable from the on-demand channel
- [ ] a cold agent session in an adopter repo can still reach the collision recipe

## Invariants

- No adopter loses content. Every byte removed from a local `AGENTS.md` is
  reachable from a command before the removal lands.
- The shipped asset stays lean: this ticket must not become the vehicle that
  makes eleven repos pay the harness's accumulated cost.
- `erg check`'s exit code is unchanged for a pristine corpus.
- Enforcing without a fresh adopter survey could fail a legitimate repo;
  the survey, not the table, is the safeguard.

## Children

This ticket is a tracker. Its Actions/Test/Verification below are superseded by
the children's and are kept for the record, not for direct execution.

1. **0288** — Actions 2-4, the content migration into the on-demand channel.
   No dependency on anything; lands first.
2. **0289** — Action 5, the hard error, plus Action 1's re-survey at enforcement
   time. `Blocked-by: 0288` (the message must name a destination that exists)
   and sequenced after 0283 (which supplies the signal that keeps "stampless"
   distinguishable from "edited").
3. Action 6 and the two harness-side verification bullets are **not** children
   of this ticket. They are actions in another repository and belong to
   ImperialDragonHarness 0906, where they are actually closable.

## Exit criteria

- 0288 and 0289 are both closed
- The design decision above is recorded here, so the next agent does not retake it
- The integration check is run and recorded in this ticket's log: in a repo that
  has only run `erg init`, the lore is reachable AND the ban fires — the two
  halves compose, rather than each passing its own child's tests alone

Deliberately **not** an exit criterion of this ticket, though it was one before
the split: "the ImperialDragonHarness copy is byte-identical to the shipped
asset" and "a cold agent session in an adopter repo can still reach the
collision recipe". Both describe state in another repository that no git-erg PR
can produce. They moved to 0906. Leaving them here made this ticket
unclosable by construction — the defect the raid's monster-ticket checklist
caught on 2026-09-16.

## Observations from the harness side (2026-09-10)

Reached independently, from the specimen repo, before this ticket was found.
They do not change the decision above; three of them are work the migration
must do anyway, and the fourth prices it.

**1. The `erg-github` sentence is a mood error, not a fiction.** The shipped
asset states in the indicative that `tickets/erg-github` "adds a `verify` check
that fails a PR referencing a still-open ticket". The helper is real — a
5 471-byte POSIX script with `install` and `verify` verbs — and does exactly
that. It is installed in **zero** repos: the script exists only in this repo's
own `tickets/`, and `erg-verify.yml` is in no workflows directory anywhere,
including this one. So every adopter reads that a required pre-merge check
exists when none does. The harness deleted the sentence in its fork under the
label "erg-github fiction", which is imprecise in the other direction. The
repair is the mood — describe an optional facility as optional, naming
`erg-github install` — not deletion.

**2. The shipped asset carries a rule the harness fork lost.** The anti-suffix
bullet ("Ticket IDs are atomic 4-digit numbers -- never suffix them (`0134a`,
`0134b`)") is in the asset and in every adopter, and absent from the harness
copy, which overwrote that bullet with its optimistic-ID material. Returning
the specimen to pristine therefore *restores* a rule as well as removing
excess; the migration is not purely subtractive on that side.

**3. A downstream rules file cites this asset by name.** The harness's
`rules/coding-bash.md` points at `tickets/AGENTS.md` as the file stating the
general form of "a check whose all-clear is indistinguishable from 'I could
not look'". Whatever destination the displaced content gets, that citation has
to be repointed in the same change, or the ban lands a dangling reference in
a resident rule body.

**4. Priced, the displaced material is smaller than the ban assumes.** The
design note above weighs folding "5 900 bytes" into the shipped asset. That is
the raw delta, and it is mostly dated incident narrative. Rewritten as rules,
with every postmortem removed and the anti-suffix bullet restored, a merged
candidate measures **3 873 bytes total** against the shipped 2 141 — an
increase of 1 730 bytes, roughly 430 resident tokens per adopter, not 1 500.
Whether that is worth paying is the author's call and the design note may still
prefer the on-demand channel; the point is only that the trade is priced
differently once the war stories are gone. The candidate was drafted for the
measurement and is reproducible from the specimen's history.

## Related

- 0278 — the asset-machinery tracker. No longer a prerequisite: its
  historical-hash child (0281) is closed wontfix, and the repo that
  justified it was measured and found already recognized.
- ImperialDragonHarness 0906 — the harness half: returning the specimen to
  pristine, relocating its forge-specific practice into that repo's own rules
  tree, and gating the result by checksum against this asset rather than by a
  size budget. Blocked on this ticket, and on the arbitration in observation 4.
